Given a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161993 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161993 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1045 "Change of Variables in Quadratic Forms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162218 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "91237c92-d7c1-506c-95d7-177c7680008d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162603 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162603 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162603 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1981982e-98dc-5f26-a882-3a23d659ddac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162603 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162852 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162852 #edb/ref 1061 30536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162852 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b50d538b-7a37-5a5b-af5f-d7d425017761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162852 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162873 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162873 #edb/ref 1061 30612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162873 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "865b4c88-60a2-55e6-b4b2-f027d0718ebe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162873 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162874 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162874 #edb/ref 1061 30613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162874 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "124e7230-af5a-57e2-95dc-9c539b7d58db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162874 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162875 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162875 #edb/ref 1061 30614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162875 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5900140f-95ae-5f3f-85f7-2351c6ab39d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162875 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162876 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162876 #edb/ref 1061 30615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3a1f8da-11aa-5561-b435-f00a5790a026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162877 #edb/ref 1061 30616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162877 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "75121285-4880-5444-b03b-36d42d881cfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162877 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163227 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\n and the matrix ![](images/13/13fad01fd9fcf327f8c45afa8264d92d9de1db135b7a87add4e43b9617574d8d.svg) that defines  the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ![](images/c6/c6f0e1bf4282bf2ffedc462005ea30ad8bbe07917bab42eaf7fedf8fd2c49ef7.svg) and ![](images/ad/ad7dec0dac6e2a50d428c7d7dbeda4e71812df2f6ac2d80d6d834ffc82f4a0cb.svg) \n\n How do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}?$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/ae/aefd4438342fcc5201fd2a3a04e4419f9c5189c4cd103eb63fa1ab6d464edc35.svg) \n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+8(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+16{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163227 #edb/ref 1065 4885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163227 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "129771b6-7bea-5c9d-b4c7-05ef8a7493b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163227 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163236 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given a quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines  the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}{P}^{T}\\,AP\\,y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}AP)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the new matrix of our quadratic form is \n\n$$\nB=  P^T \\! A P.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163236 #edb/ref 1065 4901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163236 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "61f41743-ca11-557a-b0c1-bba72921428a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163236 #edb/ref 1067 "The Connection Between Matrices of Quadratic Forms in Different Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163237 #edb/ref 1064 "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n*The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* \n\n$$\nB = P^T \\! A \\, P.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from  $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163237 #edb/ref 1065 4902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163237 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "abe7e02c-7997-5e41-9c51-98c6291198a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163237 #edb/ref 1067 "Equivalent Quadratic Forms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269279 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269279 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269279 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01bcee7d-c18b-5290-b860-ece3e5782254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269794 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269794 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269794 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39c6bc20-9e27-5b1d-b18b-f5ab734b0894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270157 #edb/ref 1080 "-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+8{y}_{2}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270157 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270157 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "640c0d02-7517-5fa1-99a0-2d4f419ec5d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270322 #edb/ref 1080 "-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "771b0292-67c0-5132-876e-28a8b44bb8b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271308 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e93ca6ed-0462-567a-8ce1-5c1a5fdcf884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271718 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "756b282c-1872-55ab-a930-f41e2431e417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272176 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272176 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272176 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272176 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272176 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fc6da69-d546-5839-88d5-382690439002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272176 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{1}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(-{y}_{2})^{2}+(-{y}_{2})({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})-({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354685 #edb/ref 1080 "-12{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354685 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354685 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03a67e30-680d-511e-bfe1-bea257f28b03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [